.TH WAV2VIRCON 1 "October 11, 2026" "Vircon32 DevTools 26.04.24" "Vircon32 Development Tools"
.SH NAME
wav2vircon \- convert a WAV sound into a Vircon32 sound
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wav2vircon
.RB [ \-o
.IR output.vsnd ]
.RB [ \-r
.IR rate ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.I input.wav
.br
.B wav2vircon
.B \-\-help
.br
.B wav2vircon
.B \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wav2vircon
converts a WAV file into a VSND sound, the format
.BR packrom (1)
puts into a cartridge. The input may be any WAV that SDL2's loader
accepts (integer PCM, float and the common compressed encodings, mono
or stereo, at any sample rate); it is
converted to the console's own format: signed 16-bit stereo samples at
44100 Hz.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " output.vsnd"
Write the sound to
.IR output.vsnd .
Without
.BR \-o ,
the output path is the input path with its extension replaced by
.IR .vsnd .
.TP
.BI \-r " rate"
Resample to
.I rate
samples per second instead of 44100, or keep the input's own rate when
.I rate
is 0. Accepted range: 1000 to 100000, or 0. The console always plays
sounds at 44100 Hz, so a sound stored at any other rate plays faster or
slower (and higher or lower) than the original unless the program sets
the channel speed to compensate (see
.BR audio.h (3v32)).
.TP
.B \-v
Verbose: report each step.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and author, and exit.
.SH OUTPUT FORMAT
A VSND file is a 12-byte header \(em the 8 characters
.BR V32\-VSND ,
then the number of samples as a little-endian 32-bit integer \(em
followed by the samples, 4 bytes each: the left and then the right
channel as little-endian signed 16-bit values.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The sound was written.
.TP
.B 1
Any error, printed as
.IR "wav2vircon: error: description" .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vircon2wav (1),
.BR packrom (1),
.BR audio.h (3v32)
.SH AUTHOR
The Vircon32 WAV importer was written by Javier Carracedo
.RI ( Carra ).
